    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nMicroservices are small, independent services that communicate over a network. They are loosely coupled, independently deployable, and organized around business capabilities. A monolith is a single, tightly coupled application where all components are deployed together.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Microservices are small, independent services that communicate over a network.\n2. They are loosely coupled, independently deployable, and organized around business capabilities.\n3. A monolith is a single, tightly coupled application where all components are deployed together.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nName the common items first and give a short purpose for each. Spend most of the time on the distinction most likely to matter in the role.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Microservices are small, independent services that communicate over a network.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Microservices are small, independent services that communicate over a network.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
