    slug: "rest-vs-grpc",
    shortDescription: "Compare the protocols.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nREST uses HTTP/1.1 with JSON, is text-based, and supports CRUD operations. gRPC uses HTTP/2 with Protocol Buffers (binary serialization), supports bidirectional streaming, and is more efficient. gRPC is ideal for internal service-to-service communication. REST is an architectural style commonly implemented over HTTP and often uses JSON; it is not inherently tied to HTTP/1.1. gRPC commonly uses HTTP/2 and Protocol Buffers and supports streaming.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between rest and grpc in microservices and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. REST uses HTTP/1.1 with JSON, is text-based, and supports CRUD operations.\n2. gRPC uses HTTP/2 with Protocol Buffers (binary serialization), supports bidirectional streaming, and is more efficient.\n3. gRPC is ideal for internal service-to-service communication.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. REST uses HTTP/1.1 with JSON, is text-based, and supports CRUD operations.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. REST uses HTTP/1.1 with JSON, is text-based, and supports CRUD operations.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
