    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA queue implements point-to-point messaging: one message is consumed by one consumer. A topic implements publish-subscribe: messages are broadcast to all subscribers. Kafka uses topics with partitions for scalability.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a queue and a topic in messaging and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A queue implements point-to-point messaging: one message is consumed by one consumer.\n2. A topic implements publish-subscribe: messages are broadcast to all subscribers.\n3. Kafka uses topics with partitions for scalability.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A queue implements point-to-point messaging: one message is consumed by one consumer.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A queue implements point-to-point messaging: one message is consumed by one consumer.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
