    slug: "cqrs",
    shortDescription: "Explain separating read and write models.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCQRS separates the models for reading (queries) and writing (commands) data. It allows optimizing each side independently – writes are often stored as events (Event Sourcing), and reads use denormalized views for performance.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain CQRS (Command Query Responsibility Segregation) accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. CQRS separates the models for reading (queries) and writing (commands) data.\n2. It allows optimizing each side independently – writes are often stored as events (Event Sourcing), and reads use denormalized views for performance.\n3. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. CQRS separates the models for reading (queries) and writing (commands) data.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. CQRS separates the models for reading (queries) and writing (commands) data.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
