    shortDescription: "Explain schema evolution.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nUse backward-compatible changes (e.g., adding columns). Perform migrations in phases: add new columns, deploy code that uses them, then remove old columns. Use online schema change tools. Each service manages its own migrations.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how do you handle database migrations in a microservices environment, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Use backward-compatible changes (e.g., adding columns).\n2. Perform migrations in phases: add new columns, deploy code that uses them, then remove old columns.\n3. Use online schema change tools.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nGive the normal path first, then mention the point where the operation can fail or become unsafe.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in a production application can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. Use backward-compatible changes (e.g., adding columns).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Use backward-compatible changes (e.g., adding columns).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
