    shortDescription: "Explain the two event types.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA domain event is internal to a bounded context (service) and represents a change in state. An integration event is published to other services to notify them of changes. Integration events enable loose coupling.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a domain event and an integration event and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A domain event is internal to a bounded context (service) and represents a change in state.\n2. An integration event is published to other services to notify them of changes.\n3. Integration events enable loose coupling.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A domain event is internal to a bounded context (service) and represents a change in state.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A domain event is internal to a bounded context (service) and represents a change in state.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
