    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA message is a generic unit of communication that can be a command or a query. An event is a message that signifies something has happened (past tense). Events are the basis of event-driven architectures.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a message and an event and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A message is a generic unit of communication that can be a command or a query.\n2. An event is a message that signifies something has happened (past tense).\n3. Events are the basis of event-driven architectures.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A message is a generic unit of communication that can be a command or a query.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A message is a generic unit of communication that can be a command or a query.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
