    shortDescription: "Explain the two types.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA hard timeout aborts the request immediately after the time limit. A soft timeout logs a warning but allows the request to continue, useful for detecting slow operations without failing them.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a hard timeout and a soft timeout and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A hard timeout aborts the request immediately after the time limit.\n2. A soft timeout logs a warning but allows the request to continue, useful for detecting slow operations without failing them.\n3. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A hard timeout aborts the request immediately after the time limit.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A hard timeout aborts the request immediately after the time limit.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
