    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt would be a separate service that receives events (e.g., user registration) and sends notifications via email, SMS, push. Use a message queue to decouple from other services. Use a template engine for message formatting. Store notification logs.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how would you design a notification service in microservices, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. It would be a separate service that receives events (e.g., user registration) and sends notifications via email, SMS, push.\n2. Use a message queue to decouple from other services.\n3. Use a template engine for message formatting.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain the procedure as a state change rather than a list of commands. Say what changes at each important step and how you would verify it.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in a production application can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. It would be a separate service that receives events (e.g., user registration) and sends notifications via email, SMS, push.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. It would be a separate service that receives events (e.g., user registration) and sends notifications via email, SMS, push.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
