    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nUse Redis (Redlock) or ZooKeeper to acquire a lock. Store a unique identifier (owner) and a TTL. Use a watchdog (background process) to extend the lock if needed. Ensure safe release to avoid deadlocks. A production design should define ownership, lease expiry, safe release, renewal, and protection against stale holders. Fencing tokens are often more important than simply adding more Redis nodes.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how would you design a distributed lock for a specific operation, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Use Redis (Redlock) or ZooKeeper to acquire a lock.\n2. Store a unique identifier (owner) and a TTL.\n3. Use a watchdog (background process) to extend the lock if needed.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain the procedure as a state change rather than a list of commands. Say what changes at each important step and how you would verify it.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in a production application can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. Use Redis (Redlock) or ZooKeeper to acquire a lock.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Use Redis (Redlock) or ZooKeeper to acquire a lock.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
