    question: "What is the difference between a service mesh and an API gateway?",
    slug: "service-mesh-vs-api-gateway",
    shortDescription: "Compare their roles.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAn API Gateway handles external client-to-service communication (east-west traffic). A service mesh handles internal service-to-service communication (north-south traffic). They can be used together. The usual terminology is north-south for external client traffic and east-west for service-to-service traffic; an API Gateway is commonly on the north-south path, while a service mesh primarily manages east-west communication. A service mesh does not replace an API Gateway: the gateway usually handles external API concerns, while the mesh focuses on internal service-to-service traffic.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a service mesh and an api gateway and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. An API Gateway handles external client-to-service communication (east-west traffic).\n2. A service mesh handles internal service-to-service communication (north-south traffic).\n3. They can be used together.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. An API Gateway handles external client-to-service communication (east-west traffic).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. An API Gateway handles external client-to-service communication (east-west traffic).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAn API Gateway handles external client-to-service communication (east-west traffic). A service mesh handles internal service-to-service communication (north-south traffic). They can be used together. The usual terminology is north-south for external client traffic and east-west for service-to-service traffic; an API Gateway is commonly on the north-south path, while a service mesh primarily manages east-west communication. A service mesh does not replace an API Gateway: the gateway usually handles external API concerns, while the mesh focuses on internal service-to-service traffic.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a service mesh and an api gateway and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. An API Gateway handles external client-to-service communication (east-west traffic).\n2. A service mesh handles internal service-to-service communication (north-south traffic).\n3. They can be used together.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. An API Gateway handles external client-to-service communication (east-west traffic).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. An API Gateway handles external client-to-service communication (east-west traffic).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
