    question: "What is the difference between a microservice and a nanoservice?",
    slug: "micro-vs-nanoservice",
    shortDescription: "Clarify the terms.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA nanoservice is an extremely small service, often with just one endpoint. It is considered an anti-pattern because it leads to excessive network overhead and complexity. Microservices are larger and more cohesive. “Nanoservice” is not a formal standard architectural category. The useful distinction is service granularity: making services excessively small can increase network hops, deployment overhead, and operational complexity.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a microservice and a nanoservice and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A nanoservice is an extremely small service, often with just one endpoint.\n2. It is considered an anti-pattern because it leads to excessive network overhead and complexity.\n3. Microservices are larger and more cohesive.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A nanoservice is an extremely small service, often with just one endpoint.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A nanoservice is an extremely small service, often with just one endpoint.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
