    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA health check determines if a service is alive. A circuit breaker is a client-side pattern that decides whether to attempt calls to a service based on recent failures. They are complementary.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a circuit breaker and a health check and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A health check determines if a service is alive.\n2. A circuit breaker is a client-side pattern that decides whether to attempt calls to a service based on recent failures.\n3. They are complementary.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A health check determines if a service is alive.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A health check determines if a service is alive.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
