    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSSR (Server-Side Rendering): HTML is generated on each request. SSG (Static Site Generation): HTML is generated at build time and reused. CSR (Client-Side Rendering): HTML is minimal and content is rendered in the browser. Next.js supports all three.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between ssr, ssg, and csr and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. SSR (Server-Side Rendering): HTML is generated on each request.\n2. SSG (Static Site Generation): HTML is generated at build time and reused.\n3. CSR (Client-Side Rendering): HTML is minimal and content is rendered in the browser.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. SSR (Server-Side Rendering): HTML is generated on each request.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. SSR (Server-Side Rendering): HTML is generated on each request.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
