    shortDescription: "Explain environment variables.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nUse `.env.local`, `.env.development`, `.env.production` files. Variables prefixed with `NEXT_PUBLIC_` are exposed to the browser. Server-side only variables (no prefix) are available in Node.js.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how do you handle environment variables in next.js, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Use `.env.local`, `.env.development`, `.env.production` files.\n2. Variables prefixed with `NEXT_PUBLIC_` are exposed to the browser.\n3. Server-side only variables (no prefix) are available in Node.js.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nGive the normal path first, then mention the point where the operation can fail or become unsafe.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in a production application can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. Use `.env.local`, `.env.development`, `.env.production` files.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Use `.env.local`, `.env.development`, `.env.production` files.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
