    slug: "edge-runtime",
    shortDescription: "Explain Edge Runtime.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe Edge Runtime runs your code on CDN edge servers (Vercel). It's used for Middleware, Edge API Routes, and Edge Functions. It has lower latency and supports Web APIs, but limited Node.js features.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the Edge Runtime accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. The Edge Runtime runs your code on CDN edge servers (Vercel).\n2. It's used for Middleware, Edge API Routes, and Edge Functions.\n3. It has lower latency and supports Web APIs, but limited Node.js features.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. The Edge Runtime runs your code on CDN edge servers (Vercel).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. The Edge Runtime runs your code on CDN edge servers (Vercel).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
