    slug: "node-vs-edge",
    shortDescription: "Compare runtimes.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nNode.js runtime is full-featured, supports all Node.js APIs. Edge Runtime is V8-based, lightweight, and runs on the edge; it supports only Web APIs and some Node.js APIs. Edge is faster but more limited.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between the node.js runtime and edge runtime and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Node.js runtime is full-featured, supports all Node.js APIs.\n2. Edge Runtime is V8-based, lightweight, and runs on the edge; it supports only Web APIs and some Node.js APIs.\n3. Edge is faster but more limited.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Node.js runtime is full-featured, supports all Node.js APIs.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Node.js runtime is full-featured, supports all Node.js APIs.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
