    shortDescription: "Define VACUUM and its purpose.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nVACUUM reclaims storage occupied by dead rows (caused by updates/deletes). It also updates statistics for the query planner. Without VACUUM, tables can bloat, leading to performance degradation. `VACUUM FULL` (or `CLUSTER`) reclaims space but locks the table; regular `VACUUM` is non-blocking.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can describe the `VACUUM` command and why it is important. accurately and connect the explanation to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. VACUUM reclaims storage occupied by dead rows (caused by updates/deletes).\n2. It also updates statistics for the query planner.\n3. Without VACUUM, tables can bloat, leading to performance degradation.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. VACUUM reclaims storage occupied by dead rows (caused by updates/deletes).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production system, this matters when engineers need the behavior described by the concept. VACUUM reclaims storage occupied by dead rows (caused by updates/deletes).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming a schema or index is automatically faster without checking access patterns.\n- Ignoring concurrency or consistency requirements.\n- Quoting performance characteristics without measured evidence.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
