    shortDescription: "Define the database object.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAn index is a data structure that improves the speed of data retrieval operations (SELECT). It is like a book index. PostgreSQL supports several index types: B-tree (default), Hash, GiST, GIN, BRIN, and SP-GiST. Indexes add overhead on INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain an index in PostgreSQL accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. An index is a data structure that improves the speed of data retrieval operations (SELECT).\n2. It is like a book index.\n3. PostgreSQL supports several index types: B-tree (default), Hash, GiST, GIN, BRIN, and SP-GiST.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor `SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = ? ORDER BY created_at DESC`, an index such as `(customer_id, created_at)` may help, but the actual benefit should be confirmed from the query plan and data distribution.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. An index is a data structure that improves the speed of data retrieval operations (SELECT).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming a schema or index is automatically faster without checking access patterns.\n- Ignoring concurrency or consistency requirements.\n- Quoting performance characteristics without measured evidence.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
