    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nB-tree: default, balanced tree for equality and range queries. GiST: generalized search tree for complex data (geometry, full-text). GIN: inverted index for array, JSONB, and full-text search. BRIN: block range index for large tables with natural ordering. SP-GiST: space-partitioned GiST for specialized data. Hash: equality only.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can describe the different index types in PostgreSQL. accurately and connect the explanation to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. B-tree: default, balanced tree for equality and range queries.\n2. GiST: generalized search tree for complex data (geometry, full-text).\n3. GIN: inverted index for array, JSONB, and full-text search.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor `SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = ? ORDER BY created_at DESC`, an index such as `(customer_id, created_at)` may help, but the actual benefit should be confirmed from the query plan and data distribution.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production system, this matters when engineers need the behavior described by the concept. B-tree: default, balanced tree for equality and range queries.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming a schema or index is automatically faster without checking access patterns.\n- Ignoring concurrency or consistency requirements.\n- Quoting performance characteristics without measured evidence.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
