    shortDescription: "Compare the two.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nB-tree supports equality and range queries (`=`, `<`, `>`, `BETWEEN`) on scalar data. GIN is designed for composite data types (arrays, JSONB, full-text) and supports containment (`@>`) and overlap (`&&`) queries. GIN is slower to update but faster for complex searches.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a b-tree and a gin index and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. B-tree supports equality and range queries (`=`, `<`, `>`, `BETWEEN`) on scalar data.\n2. GIN is designed for composite data types (arrays, JSONB, full-text) and supports containment (`@>`) and overlap (`&&`) queries.\n3. GIN is slower to update but faster for complex searches.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor `SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = ? ORDER BY created_at DESC`, an index such as `(customer_id, created_at)` may help, but the actual benefit should be confirmed from the query plan and data distribution.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. B-tree supports equality and range queries (`=`, `<`, `>`, `BETWEEN`) on scalar data.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
