    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA functional index uses an expression or function (e.g., `LOWER(col)`). It is useful for case-insensitive searches or querying on derived values. Example: `CREATE INDEX idx_lower_name ON users (LOWER(name));`.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a functional index accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A functional index uses an expression or function (e.g., `LOWER(col)`).\n2. It is useful for case-insensitive searches or querying on derived values.\n3. Example: `CREATE INDEX idx_lower_name ON users (LOWER(name));`.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor `SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = ? ORDER BY created_at DESC`, an index such as `(customer_id, created_at)` may help, but the actual benefit should be confirmed from the query plan and data distribution.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A functional index uses an expression or function (e.g., `LOWER(col)`).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming a schema or index is automatically faster without checking access patterns.\n- Ignoring concurrency or consistency requirements.\n- Quoting performance characteristics without measured evidence.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
