    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`LATERAL` allows a subquery to reference columns from preceding tables in the `FROM` clause. It is useful for writing subqueries that depend on previous rows. Example: `SELECT u.name, q.* FROM users u, LATERAL (SELECT * FROM orders WHERE user_id = u.id LIMIT 1) q;`.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `LATERAL` allows a subquery to reference columns from preceding tables in the `FROM` clause.\n2. It is useful for writing subqueries that depend on previous rows.\n3. Example: `SELECT u.name, q.* FROM users u, LATERAL (SELECT * FROM orders WHERE user_id = u.id LIMIT 1) q;`.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the requested list directly, then go deeper on the item most relevant to the interviewer’s context.\n\n**Example :**\nJoin `orders` to `customers` on `customer_id` when you need both order and customer data while keeping customer attributes normalized.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a data-backed service, what are `lateral` joins matters when correctness, access patterns, concurrency, and query or storage cost have to work together. Measure the real workload rather than assuming a design choice is always beneficial.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
