    slug: "cascade-vs-restrict",
    shortDescription: "Explain the options.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`CASCADE` automatically drops objects that depend on the dropped object (e.g., foreign keys). `RESTRICT` prevents dropping if any dependent objects exist (default). Use `CASCADE` carefully.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `cascade` and `restrict` in `drop` commands and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `CASCADE` automatically drops objects that depend on the dropped object (e.g., foreign keys).\n2. `RESTRICT` prevents dropping if any dependent objects exist (default).\n3. Use `CASCADE` carefully.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `CASCADE` automatically drops objects that depend on the dropped object (e.g., foreign keys).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `CASCADE` automatically drops objects that depend on the dropped object (e.g., foreign keys).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
