    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nRLS allows restricting which rows a user can access based on a policy. It is used for fine-grained access control. Enable RLS on a table: `ALTER TABLE table_name ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;`. Then create policies using `CREATE POLICY`.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. RLS allows restricting which rows a user can access based on a policy.\n2. It is used for fine-grained access control.\n3. Enable RLS on a table: `ALTER TABLE table_name ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;`.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nName the common items first and give a short purpose for each. Spend most of the time on the distinction most likely to matter in the role.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, an engineer can apply the control to an actual request or resource boundary and verify that an unauthorized action is rejected. RLS allows restricting which rows a user can access based on a policy.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a data-backed service, what are row-level security (rls) policies matters when correctness, access patterns, concurrency, and query or storage cost have to work together. Measure the real workload rather than assuming a design choice is always beneficial.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
