    shortDescription: "Compare the two.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nPhysical replication replicates the entire cluster at a binary level (WAL). Logical replication replicates changes at the table level, allowing selective replication, filtering, and cross-version replication. Physical is lower-level and more robust.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between logical and physical replication and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Physical replication replicates the entire cluster at a binary level (WAL).\n2. Logical replication replicates changes at the table level, allowing selective replication, filtering, and cross-version replication.\n3. Physical is lower-level and more robust.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Physical replication replicates the entire cluster at a binary level (WAL).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Physical replication replicates the entire cluster at a binary level (WAL).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
