    slug: "jsonb-advantages",
    shortDescription: "Explain the benefits.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nJSONB provides flexibility for schema-less data, reduces complexity for nested structures, and allows for fast queries when indexed. It is ideal for storing semi-structured data from external APIs.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks engineering judgment around what are the advantages of using jsonb over relational columns. A useful answer makes the benefit, cost, and condition for choosing the approach explicit.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. JSONB provides flexibility for schema-less data, reduces complexity for nested structures, and allows for fast queries when indexed.\n2. It is ideal for storing semi-structured data from external APIs.\n3. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what you gain, what you give up, and what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. JSONB provides flexibility for schema-less data, reduces complexity for nested structures, and allows for fast queries when indexed.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a data-backed service, what are the advantages of using jsonb over relational columns matters when correctness, access patterns, concurrency, and query or storage cost have to work together. Measure the real workload rather than assuming a design choice is always beneficial.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Listing benefits and drawbacks without saying when they matter.\n- Treating one approach as universally superior.\n- Ignoring operational cost, maintenance, or failure behavior.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nName the constraint that makes the trade-off acceptable, then state when you would deliberately choose another approach.",
