    question: "What is the difference between a product demo and a POC?",
    slug: "demo-vs-poc",
    shortDescription: "Compare demo and POC.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA demo is a high-level presentation showing product capabilities, often pre-configured. A POC (Proof of Concept) is a hands-on validation with customer-specific data or scenarios, proving technical feasibility. In an interview, I would explain this by connecting the concept to the real PreSales workflow. The important point is not only what the concept means, but how I would apply it with a customer, how I would make decisions, what risks I would consider, and what outcome I would expect. I would begin with the customer's business objective and constraints, then translate the requirement into a practical technical or commercial approach. If several approaches are possible, I would compare them using business value, technical feasibility, cost, risk, timeline, security, scalability, maintainability, and customer impact rather than choosing an option only because it has more features. I.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis is asked to see whether you can turn a customer requirement into a technically credible recommendation. The interviewer is looking for assumptions, trade-offs, validation steps, and a clear link between business need and solution.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A demo is a high-level presentation showing product capabilities, often pre-configured.\n2. A POC (Proof of Concept) is a hands-on validation with customer-specific data or scenarios, proving technical feasibility.\n3. In an interview, I would explain this by connecting the concept to the real PreSales workflow.\n4. State the decision criteria and the evidence or documentation that supports the decision..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a customer solution discussion, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A demo is a high-level presentation showing product capabilities, often pre-configured.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn pre-sales, the concept matters when matching a customer requirement to a technically credible solution. The recommendation should state assumptions, integration constraints, risks, and what would need to be validated in a proof of concept.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
